Here's an example SystemVerilog module for an LDPC (Low-Density Parity Check) encoder for an FPGA implementation:

```verilog
module ldpc_encoder(
    input wire clk,
    input wire reset,
    input wire data_in,
    output wire data_out
);

reg [7:0] data_reg;
reg [3:0] parity_reg;

always @(posedge clk or posedge reset)
begin
    if (reset)
    begin
        data_reg <= 8'b00000000;
        parity_reg <= 4'b0000;
    end
    else
    begin
        data_reg <= data_in;
        parity_reg <= parity_calc(data_reg);
        data_out <= data_reg[7:4] | (parity_reg[3:0] << 4);
    end
end

function [4:0] parity_calc(data);
    parity_calc <= data[7:4] ^ data[3:0];
endfunction : parity_calc

endmodule : ldpc_encoder
```

This module implements a simple LDPC encoder with a 4-bit parity check. The `ldpc_encoder` module has four inputs: `clk`, `reset`, `data_in`, and `data_out`. The `clk` and `reset` signals are used to control the operation of the module, while the `data_in` signal is used to provide the data to be encoded. The `data_out` signal is used to output the encoded data.

The `ldpc_encoder` module contains two registers: `data_reg` and `parity_reg`. The `data_reg` register is used to store the input data, while the `parity_reg` register is used to store the parity information. The `parity_calc` function is used to calculate the parity information based on the input data.

The `always` block is used to implement the encoding logic. When the `clk` or `reset` signal changes, the `data_reg` and `parity_reg` registers are updated

