Certainly! Here's a simple JavaScript function that takes a sentence as input and returns a new sentence with the adjectives reordered:

```javascript
function switchAdjectives(sentence) {
  // Split the sentence into an array of words
  const words = sentence.split(" ");

  // Initialize two index variables: start and end
  let start = 0;
  let end = words.length - 1;

  // While start is less than end...
  while (start < end) {
    // Get the current word at the start and end indices
    const startWord = words[start];
    const endWord = words[end];

    // If the current word is an adjective (i.e., it contains only letters), swap it with the word at the other index
    if (/^[a-zA-Z]+$/.test(startWord)) {
      words[start] = endWord;
      words[end] = startWord;
    }

    // Move the start index forward and the end index backward
    start++;
    end--;
  }

  // Join the words back into a sentence and return it
  return words.join(" ");
}

// Test the function with your example sentence
const originalSentence = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const reorderedSentence = switchAdjectives(originalSentence);
console.log(reorderedSentence); // Output: "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

This function uses a simple algorithm to iterate through the sentence, checking each word for adjectiveness (in this case, using a regular expression to check for only alphabetic characters). If a word is an adjective, it is swapped with the word at the other index. The function then moves the start and end indices forward or backward until all the adjectives have been swapped. Finally, the function joins the words back into a sentence and returns it.

