Certainly! Here's a simple MATLAB code that simulates a basic scenario of natural selection:

```matlab
% Define the initial population
population = [1 2 3 4 5]; % Individuals
trait = [0 1 0 1 0]; % Trait values (e.g., 0 for disadvantageous, 1 for advantageous)

% Set up the selection process
selection_pressure = 0.5; % Probability of selecting individuals with advantageous trait

% Simulate the evolution over time
num_generations = 100;
for i = 1:num_generations
    % Select individuals based on the trait value
    selected_individuals = population(randperm(length(population), round(length(population) * selection_pressure)));

    % Create the next generation by reproducing selected individuals
    next_generation = selected_individuals .* trait;

    % Update the population for the next generation
    population = next_generation;
end

% Plot the frequency of the advantageous trait over time
figure;
plot(1:num_generations, mean(trait));
xlabel('Generation');
ylabel('Frequency of Advantageous Trait');
title('Natural Selection Simulation');
```

This code simulates a simple scenario where there is a selective pressure favoring individuals with a certain trait (in this case, 1). The population evolves over time as individuals with the advantageous trait are more likely to be selected for reproduction. The plot shows how the frequency of the advantageous trait changes over time, which is a classic example of natural selection at work.

