A logical syllogism is a type of argument that consists of two premises and a conclusion. The premises provide information about the subject, while the conclusion draws a conclusion based on those premises. A well-formed syllogism must have a valid structure and use logical reasoning to connect the premises with the conclusion. Here's an example of a logical syllogism:

Premise 1: All humans are mortal. (Humanity is a subset of Mortality)
Premise 2: Socrates is human. (Socrates is an instance of Humanity)
Conclusion: Therefore, Socrates is mortal. (If Socrates is human and humanity is a subset of Mortality, then Socrates must be mortal)
This syllogism works by using the first premise to establish a relationship between humanity and mortality. The second premise then identifies Socrates as a member of the humanity set. By combining these two premises, we can conclude that Socrates must also be mortal because he belongs to the same set as all other humans, which is mortal.
In Latex, this syllogism can be represented as follows:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{array}{l}
\text{Premise 1:} \quad \text{All humans are mortal.} \\
\text{Premise 2:} \quad \text{Socrates is human.} \\
\text{Conclusion:} \quad \text{Therefore, Socrates is mortal.}
\end{array}
\end{equation*}
This representation uses the \texttt{equation*} environment to display the syllogism in a formatted way. Each line represents a separate part of the syllogism, and the \texttt{\\} command is used to separate the lines. The text within each line is enclosed in curly braces (\texttt{\{} and \texttt{\}}), which allows us to specify the text that we want to display.

