To make the planets less close together, you can introduce a space between each planet when generating them randomly. You can achieve this by calculating the distance between each planet and the previous one before adding it to the array. If the distance is too small, you can generate a new random position until the distance is acceptable. Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Calculate distance between current planet and previous ones
    const distances = planets.map((prevPlanet) => {
      const dx = planet.position[0] - prevPlanet.position[0];
      const dy = planet.position[1] - prevPlanet.position[1];
      const dz = planet.position[2] - prevPlanet.position[2];
      return Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy + dz * dz);
    });

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = distances.some((distance) => distance < minDistance);

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

Make sure to update the `minDistance` parameter according to your desired spacing between planets.

